---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Intro from '../../components/case/Intro.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/case/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/case/Figure.astro';
import LoopVideo from '../../components/LoopVideo.astro';
import ExplorationFooter from '../../components/case/ExplorationFooter.astro';
import poster from '../../assets/explorations/halden/poster.png';
import video from '../../assets/explorations/halden/video-1280.mp4';
import overview from '../../assets/explorations/halden/01-overview.png';
import mobileWall from '../../assets/explorations/halden/02-mobile-wall.png';
import heroDevices from '../../assets/explorations/halden/03-hero-devices.png';
import collection from '../../assets/explorations/halden/04-collection.png';
import woods from '../../assets/explorations/halden/05-woods.png';
import mobileScreens from '../../assets/explorations/halden/06-mobile-screens.png';
import product from '../../assets/explorations/halden/07-product.png';

// Copy and shots come from Figma node 192:7481 "Sheet — Halden (full scroll)".
// Shots are exported at 3x (1920 × 1440); the hero is a 1280 × 960 video.
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Halden"
  description="A landing page for a made-to-order furniture studio in the Hudson Valley. Concept work for an invented brand."
>
  <Intro
    title="Halden"
    tags={['Landing page', 'Desktop + mobile', 'Oct 2026']}
    lede="A landing page for a made-to-order furniture studio in the Hudson Valley. Concept work for an invented brand: I wanted the photography to carry the page, so the type stays quiet and a line only appears where two things meet."
  >
    <LoopVideo
      src={video}
      poster={poster}
      label="A scroll through the Halden landing page on desktop and mobile."
      width={1280}
      height={960}
      class="hero-video"
    />
  </Intro>

  <Chapter title="Challenge">
    <p class="p">
      Nobody spends close to five thousand dollars on a chair they have never touched without some hesitation. In a
      showroom that hesitation is handled by the room itself: the light is controlled, each piece has space around it,
      and you can walk up and check the grain with your hand. Online, all of that is gone and the buyer is left with a
      photo and a price.
    </p>
    <p class="p">
      Most furniture sites respond by showing more. More products per row, more badges, more offers. That works for a
      $200 shelf, but it works against a made-to-order piece, because a crowded page tells the buyer this is ordinary
      stock. The real problem was how to give one chair the same attention on a screen that it gets on a showroom
      floor, without hiding the practical details a serious buyer still needs, like price, size and lead time.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <div class="shots">
    <Figure src={overview} alt="Halden desktop sections side by side: the hero, the workshop statement, the Hale chair page, the collection and the rooms." />
    <Figure src={mobileWall} alt="Halden mobile screens on a dark background: the hero, the Hale chair, the collection list and the menu." />
  </div>

  <Chapter title="Solution">
    <p class="p">
      I built the page around the idea that each screen should hold one thing. Every section is roughly one viewport
      tall and carries a single idea, so the buyer looks at one chair, then one list, then one room, the way they would
      move through a showroom.
    </p>
    <p class="p">
      The photography does the job the hand would normally do. Each photo shows one piece, lit from the side so the
      grain is visible, and cropped close enough that the hero chair runs off the edge of the screen. I placed the chair
      in front of the brand name on purpose, because the product should be the first thing you read, not the logo.
    </p>
    <p class="p">
      For type I used only two levels and left out everything in between. A thin serif carries the names and statements
      and never goes below 40px, while a sans handles prices, sizes and menus and never goes above 16px. That gap is
      what makes the hierarchy obvious, and it let me keep the practical information on the page without it competing
      with the furniture.
    </p>
    <p class="p">
      The collection was the decision I went back and forth on most. A grid would show more products at once, but I
      chose a plain list of names where the photo appears on hover. It shows less at a glance, and I accepted that,
      because someone buying at this price is comparing two or three pieces, not browsing forty.
    </p>
    <p class="p">
      Phones have no hover, so I rebuilt that section for mobile as swipe cards with the next one peeking in, moved the
      price and the main action to the bottom of the screen where the thumb rests, and made every button full width.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <div class="shots">
    <Figure src={heroDevices} alt="The Halden hero on a desktop screen with the mobile hero beside it, over a room photo." />
    <Figure src={collection} alt="The collection section: a list of piece names with the photo of the hovered piece on the right." />
    <Figure src={woods} alt="The wood and finish swatches: paper, bone, walnut, olive, espresso and ink, next to a black walnut close-up." />
    <Figure src={mobileScreens} alt="Halden mobile screens: the hero, the Hale chair page with its configure button, and the menu." />
    <Figure src={product} alt="The Hale lounge chair page with detail photos of the wood and upholstery below it." />
  </div>

  <ExplorationFooter current="halden" />
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .shots {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
  }
  :global(.hero-video) {
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    border-radius: 16px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
</style>
